/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - An engine compressor raises the pressure and temperature of air before combustion.
   - At low engine power the LP stage cannot supply enough pressure.
   - Three sources feed the manifold: engine bleed (HP or LP), the auxiliary power unit (APU) on the ground and during engine start, and a ground cart connected at a ground service port.
   - The first heat exchanger is the precooler.
   - Next is the pressure regulating and shut-off valve (PRV).
   - The regulated air enters a manifold: a network of ducts that runs along the fuselage.
   - Protection is layered.
   - The manifold feeds several users, each through its own valve:
   - Cabin pressure is not a bleed user of its own: the packs feed the cabin and the outflow valve sets its pressure.
   - On a typical twin-engine aircraft, either engine can supply the whole manifold through the isolation valves.
*/
